WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.280
What happens when you combine the fiercest
animal of the sky with the proudest of the land?

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.880
You get the Griffin.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.560
This half-lion, half-eagle creature adorns
royal crests or perched menacingly atop buildings.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.080
Stories of this beast predate written language.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.800
Artifacts from cultures&nbsp;
like the Ancient Egyptians,&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.560
Assyrians, Myceneans,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.680
Babylonians, Syrians, Persians, and Greeks
bear evidence of the majestic griffin.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.400
I have a soft spot for these monsters, they
are part bird after all.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.880
But their avian majesty is only part of their
allure.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.480
Powerful, vigilant, a symbol of royalty, and
companion to gods, the griffin’s grandeur

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.000
is only seconded by its omnipresence in ancient
civilizations.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.600
Yet we’ve largely let this monster fade
into antiquity.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.640
Why did ancient peoples believe that these&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.360
creatures actually roamed&nbsp;
the earth protecting gold?

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.160
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka, and this is Monstrum.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.360
The Griffin has talon feet, a long tail,&nbsp;
an eagle-like beak, and usually wings.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.960
Known by many names across the globe, the
griffin is a bit unusual

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.280
as monster mythology goes; it&nbsp;
doesn’t have an origin story.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.600
There are no tales connecting its creation&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.760
to any deity, or heroes&nbsp;
battling it in some epic quest.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:23.680
Griffins were just animals; something people
believed they might happen across one day.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.120
That doesn’t mean they didn’t have a place
in folkloric traditions though.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.160
The griffin was said to cohabitate with others
of its kind, roaming in pairs or packs, hunting

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.400
large mammals like horses and deer—

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.960
and as monsters tend to do, the occasional human.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.840
Griffins lay eggs in nests on the ground and&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.800
primarily get around on four&nbsp;
legs, despite the wings.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.400
Since the earliest versions of griffins have
no written record, it’s hard to draw any

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.320
definitive conclusions about why they started
appearing in the first place.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.720
Nevertheless, we begin to see the Griffin&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.520
appear in cultural artifacts&nbsp;
thousands of years ago.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.560
First, in the late fourth millennium BCE in&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.320
Egypt and Elam, and in the&nbsp;
second millennium in Syria.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.920
The hybrid creatures appear on seals, amulets,
and tomb decorations and likely evoked protection.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.760
Think about it: if the lion&nbsp;
is seen as “king” of land&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.640
animals for its ferocity and beauty, and the

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.760
“eagle” as aerial nobility for the same reasons,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.720
a lion-eagle combo animal logically would&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.080
be the king of the beasts - a&nbsp;
majestic and powerful being.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.800
Who wouldn’t want to adopt&nbsp;
some of that same power?

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.120
During the Bronze Age Egyptian iconography&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.480
played up the duality of the&nbsp;
monster even more overtly -

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.320
adding crests, neck curls meant
to evoke the lion’s mane, and raised wings.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.040
Griffins begin to appear in Egyptian funerary&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.800
imagery in royal tombs as far&nbsp;
back as the Fifth Dynasty.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.080
And by the Eighteenth Dynasty, the often male
griffin, overwhelmingly symbolized the protection

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.680
and authority of the pharaohs - alongside
the female sphinx.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.320
Griffin lore enters written history sometime&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.320
between the 8th and 6th century&nbsp;
BCE in Greek literature.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.640
The Greeks even gave the griffin their name.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.920
The Greek word for curved or hooked became
the Latin grȳps, which forms the base of

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.840
the French and Italian words.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.400
Griffin is also linked to giriften, the Persian
word for “grip” or “seize.”

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.080
This etymology points to the creature’s
curved beak and talons—its most powerful weapons.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.800
The Greek stories were originally adapted
from tales told by the Scythians, a nomadic,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.840
culturally advanced people with a rich oral
tradition, who traveled a large swathe of

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.600
territory between Southeastern Europe, Central
Asia, and Mongolia.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.280
We see the griffin depicted in Scythian art,
the creatures immortalized in gold alongside

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.280
horses, stags, and eagles.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.560
They even appear as tattoos on mummified remains
of the fiercely admired Scythian warriors,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.640
indicating their cultural importance.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:53.440
One early example of the griffin appearing
in a written narrative comes from Aristeas

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.880
a Greek traveler who wrote an epic poem about
the Scythian nomads.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.080
In his story, the Scythians fight off lion
- sized creatures with curved, eagle-like

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.000
beaks as they prospect for gold.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.800
We’ve lost the complete poem to time, but
from the fragments, we get one of the griffin’s

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:15.040
defining characteristics - their connection
to gold, more specifically, to guarding gold

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.720
deposits in Central Asia.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.640
Now we’re getting somewhere—no longer
just an impressive, exotic animal, the griffin

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.480
was linked to an important cultural resource,
a precious metal tied to religion, art, currency,

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:29.440
and social power.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:36.080
Ancient history, nomadic tribes, gold…it
all made for great stories to the Greeks.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.680
In 430 BCE, Greek historian Herodotus wrote
of “griffins that guard gold” in the mountains

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.960
beyond the Issedones, located in Central Asia,
which the Greeks would have perceived as a

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.280
far-away, nearly mythical place.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.560
In the 5th century BCE, Ctesias’ Indica
gives us the first detailed written account

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.240
of the griffin’s appearance.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:01.760
He describes large gold deposits in the mountains
of India inhabited by “a race of four-footed

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.360
birds, about as large as wolves, having legs
and claws like those of the lion, and covered

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.160
all over the body with black feathers except
only on the breast where they are red.”

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:18.000
In the 3rd century CE, Greek author Aelian
gives another detailed account of griffins.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.160
Like Ctesaias’s earlier description, they&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.360
have black and red feathers,&nbsp;
but their wings are white.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.280
They will attack those who approach their
mountain nests, but not in defense of the

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.360
luminous metal but their young - making them
more vigilant and

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.920
dedicated parents rather than treasure-mongers.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.520
These are only a few examples, as the Greeks
and Romans circulated these Scythian griffin

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.880
stories the creature’s appearance in their
art increased as well.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.400
Vases, mosaics, bronze busts, bowls—the
griffin was a popular adornment.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.360
In Greek mythology, the sun-god Apollo’s
chariot was driven by griffins and Dionysus

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.360
had one to help pull his chariot as well.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.640
Zeus kept one as a companion, the helmet of
Athena is sometimes depicted adorned with

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.560
griffins, and Nemesis,&nbsp;
goddess of divine retribution,&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.520
used these defenders to punish treasure-stealers.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.280
So, the griffin already had quite the reputation,
to say the least,

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.680
even before they even made it to the Middle Ages,

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.800
when illustrated bestiaries became a bit of a fad.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.840
Just look at all these sweet griffins.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.720
Given their popularity and their acceptance
as real animals, it’s not super surprising

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.960
that beginning in the 11th to 13th centuries,
the time of the Crusades,

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.920
the griffin appeared in European heraldry.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.400
I mean—the whole protector of the young
aka the vulnerable, the features of an effective

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.080
hunter, and the whole gold&nbsp;
thing was pretty appealing.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.440
The griffin went on to grace countless seals,
shields, and armor of wealthy and noble European

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:49.600
families across countries like Germany, France,
Great Britain, and Norway.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:54.240
Money, power, a position as guardians…you
probably won’t be surprised to hear that

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.920
Christianity also adopted the griffin in their
art and literature.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.080
Isidore of Seville’s Etymologies from the&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.280
6th century painted the&nbsp;
griffin as a symbol of Christ.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:05.680
In Dante’s Divine Comedy, a griffin appears&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.720
often, receiving praise&nbsp;
for avoiding the temptation

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:13.440
of eating the forbidden fruit and pulling
a divine chariot that symbolizes the Church.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.200
Dante’s griffin, Christ-like,&nbsp;
even ascends into heaven.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:24.000
The hippogriff, a horse-lion-eagle creature,
is the offspring of a mare and a griffin.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.000
Interestingly, the hippogriff&nbsp;
is also a symbol for Christ -

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.040
believed to evoke the union&nbsp;
of the divine and the natural.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:36.400
Outside of religion, stories of the griffin’s
real existence continued into the 14th century

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:40.960
when Sir John Mandeville, who may or may not
have been an actual person, published what

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.000
he claimed to be an accurate travel memoir.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.800
Mandeville writes of a country called ‘Bacharia,’
located somewhere in “The East,”

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.280
that had more griffins than any other country.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.320
These griffins “hath the body greater and&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.920
is more strong than eight&nbsp;
lions” and a “hundred eagles.”

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:01.120
They can carry off a horse or two oxen at once.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.160
Their claws are so large, men can “make
cups of them” and their ribs and wing bones

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:07.520
can be used to make bows.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.280
In the 17th century, scholars began proposing
that the griffin was an imaginary creature—

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.560
pure symbolic fantasy - the result of overactive
imaginations and nothing more.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.280
That’s not to say everyone was convinced
griffins were entirely fictional.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.240
A Scottish minister, Andrew Ross, for instance,
wrote that there are “many such ‘mixt

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.720
and dubious’ animals in the world” so
it’s possible that griffins could also exist.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.680
In the 21st century, it’s been hypothesized&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:39.040
that griffins were in a way&nbsp;
inspired by living animals.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:41.440
Ones that happened to be&nbsp;
dead for millions of years.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:43.680
Remember the Scythians?

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.240
It’s extremely likely that across their
desert routes in what is now the Gobi desert,

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.120
they encountered bones—bones of the humans
and animals that perished in the sands,

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.160
and also fossilized prehistoric bones.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.640
Unfamiliar bones of unusual size.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:04.160
Shifting sands and frequent dust could reveal
these bones, the white standing out against

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.480
the red sand, suddenly and unexpectedly.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:08.960
What can also be exposed by high winds?

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:13.520
Mineral deposits—like gold, likely linking
the valuable resource

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:15.280
to the monster from the very beginning.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.120
The migratory routes of the Scythians in what
is now Mongolia and north-Western China have

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.760
proven to be a mecca for well-preserved fossil&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.120
skeletons, particularly&nbsp;
those of the Protoceratops.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.600
Folklorist and historian Adrienne Mayor was
the first to present the theory that the griffin

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:34.080
was born out of the discovery of these dinosaur&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.360
bones, and while some&nbsp;
paleontologists aren’t so sure,

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:38.880
it’s a compelling hypothesis.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:45.520
From Egyptian art to ancient nomadic warriors,
Greek myths to the heraldry of kings,

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.320
and pretty much everything in between, the griffin&nbsp;

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:51.840
has been a part of human lore&nbsp;
for a very, very long time.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:55.440
And while they’ve largely faded&nbsp;
out of modern popular culture

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.160
there is no escaping the fact that these creatures

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:00.720
will always be part of our human history.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.280
You underestimate me . . . Cherry-ot

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:06.000
She clearly is a master of pronunciation.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.440
Did I say "allure" wrong?

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.240
A - lure

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:08.960
A-lurr

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:09.120
A-lewer

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:09.600
Allure

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:09.680
Aww

